## Contract

* `StudentRecord.sol`

## 1. Overview

This version of the student record contract builds on the previous student registry by introducing additional Solidity features.

The contract now uses OpenZeppelin's `Ownable` contract for access control, events to record important actions, and an interface called `IStudentRecord` to define the structure of the student lookup function.

Student records contain a name, enrolment ID, and status. Registration is protected against duplicate addresses, while changing a student's status is restricted to the contract owner.

## 2. Main components

### Student status

The contract uses an enum with three possible values:

```solidity
enum Status {
    Active,
    Inactive,
    Graduated
}
```

Each student is stored using the `Student` struct:

```solidity
struct Student {
    string name;
    uint256 enrolmentId;
    Status status;
}
```

The student data and registration status are maintained using two mappings.

## 3. Interface

I created the `IStudentRecord` interface to define the `getStudent()` function that can be used to retrieve a student's information.

```solidity
interface IStudentRecord {
    function getStudent(address studentAddress)
        external
        view
        returns (
            string memory,
            uint256,
            uint8
        );
}
```

The main contract implements this interface:

```solidity
contract StudentRecord is IStudentRecord, Ownable
```

The `override` keyword is used in `getStudent()` because the function is implementing the function declared in the interface.

The status is converted to `uint8` when returned so that it matches the return type specified by the interface.

## 4. Access control

I used OpenZeppelin's `Ownable` contract rather than creating the ownership system manually.

The constructor initializes the contract owner using:

```solidity
constructor() Ownable(msg.sender) {}
```

This makes the deploying account the initial owner.

The `updateStatus()` function uses:

```solidity
onlyOwner
```

Therefore, only the contract owner can change the status of a registered student.

## 5. Custom modifier

I also created a modifier called `Alreadyregistered` to prevent duplicate registrations.

```solidity
modifier Alreadyregistered(address studentaddress) {
    require(
        !isRegistered[studentaddress],
        "Student already registered"
    );
    _;
}
```

The modifier is applied to `registerStudent()`.

Before a new student is added, the contract checks whether the supplied address has already been registered. If it has, the transaction is rejected.

## 6. Events

Two events are included in the contract:

```solidity
event Registered(string name, uint256 enrolmentId);
event update(Status newStatus);
```

The `Registered` event is emitted whenever a new student is successfully added.

The `update` event is emitted when the owner changes the status of an existing student.

Events provide a way for applications or other off-chain systems to observe important contract activity without changing the stored data.

